# The offsets the whole descent under a fusion cannot see, counted two ways. One row per fusion
# that has a resolved part: how deep its descent goes, how many fusions and how many leaf layers it
# reaches, the offsets the root's one figure cannot see, and the sum of the fusions' own offsets
# below it.
#
# What is counted
#   One level of composition sends a fusion's parts to its figure. Nesting stacks those levels, so
#   the leaf layers under a root reach that root's one figure through every level between them, and
#   `composition/descent.sqlc` walks it. Because every level reaches the whole of its figure, the
#   offsets the root cannot see are those of every level added together, so the descent under a
#   root owes two routes to one number, and this relation reports both.
#
# `closure_kernel` is `leaves - 1`
#   The root is one figure, and every level below it reaches the whole of its figure, so the leaves
#   reach the root's figure and everything else about them is an offset it cannot see.
#   `rank/composition_image.sqlc` is where each level is held to reaching the whole of its figure;
#   without it, this column counts nothing.
#
# The other route adds the fusions' offsets, each fusion once
#   Each fusion below the root adds its own offsets once, however many paths arrive at it, so the
#   walk is reduced to one row per reached layer before anything is summed. That reduction is
#   deliberate, and it gives the two routes something to disagree about: a layer reached twice
#   raises `sum_kernel` through the fusion that reached it a second time, while leaving the leaf
#   count exactly where it was.
#
# So the two agree exactly where no layer is reached twice
#   Where no layer is reached by two paths and nothing loops, the two counts are equal. A second
#   path to a leaf, or a loop, sets them apart: `checks/jagged_layer.sqlc` accuses the first and
#   `checks/layers_move_together.sqlc` reports the second, so this relation is a third check over
#   both, reading the whole descent rather than the siblings.
#
# Every leaf counts toward the root, or its factor is unstated
#   `row_dim` is 1: the root reads its leaves as one figure, each leaf weighted by the product of
#   the factors on its path, which `composition/descent.sqlc` carries as it walks. Every factor on a
#   path is above zero, so every leaf's weight is too, and the descent keeps what one level has: no
#   assignment of non-negative amounts to the leaves is invisible to the root. `leaves_positive`
#   and `leaves_unknown` count that, and together they must cover the leaves. A leaf reached through
#   a part whose factor is a typed blank is in the second column, because nobody has said what its
#   path multiplies by, and that is a different fact from a weight of zero.
#
# A fusion nothing resolved is a leaf here, and that is right
#   `rank/composition_kernel.sqlc` has no row for a fusion whose every part names a filing this
#   corpus does not hold, so nothing descends through it, and it is counted among the leaves. That
#   is what the arithmetic sees: no parts under it, nothing below it to reach.
#   `rank/composition_image.sqlc` is where the state itself is reported.
#
# Every fusion is a root of its own descent
#   Not only the fusions nothing composes. A fusion in the middle of a stack has a descent under it
#   and owes the same agreement, so keying this on the tops alone would leave the middle untested,
#   and would report one defect once per ancestor above it.
#
# Probe
#   `algebra/composition_closure.sqlc` holds the two routes together. In a rolled-back transaction,
#   give a fusion a part naming a layer already reachable beneath it through another composition:
#   the offsets summed rise, the leaf count does not, and this is the relation that says so.
-- composition/descent.sqlc reduced to one row per reached layer,
-- against rank/composition_kernel.sqlc.
SELECT r.composition,
       r.composed_layer,
       coalesce(b.deepest, 0)                             AS deepest,
       1 + coalesce(b.fusions, 0)                         AS fusions_below,
       coalesce(b.leaves, 0)                              AS leaves,
       1::bigint                                          AS row_dim,
       coalesce(b.leaves, 0) - 1                          AS closure_kernel,
       r.kernel_dim + coalesce(b.kernel, 0)               AS sum_kernel,
       coalesce(b.leaves_positive, 0)                     AS leaves_positive,
       coalesce(b.leaves_unknown, 0)                      AS leaves_unknown
FROM      (
    :compose(rank/composition_kernel.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT w.root_filing,
           w.root_layer,
           max(w.depth)::bigint                                  AS deepest,
           count(*) FILTER (WHERE k.composition IS NOT NULL)      AS fusions,
           count(*) FILTER (WHERE k.composition IS NULL)          AS leaves,
           count(*) FILTER (WHERE k.composition IS NULL
                                  AND NOT w.factor_absent
                                  AND w.factor_mode > 0)          AS leaves_positive,
           count(*) FILTER (WHERE k.composition IS NULL
                                  AND w.factor_absent)            AS leaves_unknown,
           coalesce(sum(k.kernel_dim), 0)::bigint                 AS kernel
    FROM      (
        SELECT d.root_filing, d.root_layer, d.filing, d.layer,
               max(d.depth)             AS depth,
               bool_or(d.factor_absent) AS factor_absent,
               min(d.factor_mode)       AS factor_mode
        FROM (
            :compose(composition/descent.sqlc)
        ) d
        GROUP BY d.root_filing, d.root_layer, d.filing, d.layer
    ) w
    LEFT JOIN (
        :compose(rank/composition_kernel.sqlc)
    ) k ON k.composition = w.filing AND k.composed_layer = w.layer
    GROUP BY w.root_filing, w.root_layer
) b ON b.root_filing = r.composition AND b.root_layer = r.composed_layer
